### The Board Selection Laboratory 🧪

Every great roboticist analyzes the bill of materials before picking their microcontroller board. Let's see how changing our project goals changes our board choice!

```cpp
// Monster Lab Board Decision Algorithm
void setup() {
  Serial.begin(9600);

  int requiredMotors = 6;       // 6 DC motors (2 pins each = 12 pins)
  int requiredSensors = 8;      // 8 Analog distance sensors
  int requiredServos = 4;       // 4 PWM steering servos

  int totalDigitalNeeded = (requiredMotors * 2) + requiredServos; // 16 digital pins!
  int totalAnalogNeeded = requiredSensors;                        // 8 analog pins!

  Serial.print("Digital Pins Needed: ");
  Serial.println(totalDigitalNeeded);
  Serial.print("Analog Pins Needed: ");
  Serial.println(totalAnalogNeeded);

  if (totalDigitalNeeded > 14 || totalAnalogNeeded > 6) {
    Serial.println("Verdict: Arduino Uno lacks enough pins. Deploy the Arduino Mega 2560!");
  } else {
    Serial.println("Verdict: Arduino Uno or Nano handles this with ease!");
  }
}

void loop() {
  // Idle
}
```

#### Engineering Reflection:
1. If you reduce the project to 1 motor and 1 light sensor, which board would fit neatly inside a tiny robot shell? (The **Arduino Nano**!)
2. If you need 20 pushbuttons for an escape room puzzle console, which board has enough pins without special multiplexer chips? (The **Arduino Mega**!)
